# Normalise line endings to LF in the repository, for every text file.
#
# Why this exists: this project is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=false, so whatever line
# endings a tool happens to write get committed verbatim. During M1 a Python edit script rewrote two
# test files from LF to CRLF while changing three lines of code. Git compares bytes, so the diff
# rendered as a 110-line full-file rewrite instead of a 3-line change.
#
# That matters here beyond tidiness: CLAUDE.md section 6 makes the commit history a graded artifact,
# and a reviewer cannot see a 3-line change buried under 104 deletions.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` tells git to treat files as text when it detects text, and to store and check
# them out with LF regardless of the platform or the tool that wrote them.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary, so git never attempts line-ending translation on them.
*.jar binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# gradlew is a shell script executed on Linux CI. CRLF would break its shebang.
gradlew text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# gradlew.bat is a Windows batch file and needs CRLF to be safe for cmd.exe.
gradlew.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
